Recursive debugging.

    bocl> (quit)


    Debugger invoked on condition of type SIMPLE-ERROR:
      In evale: ERROR: Undefined function: QUIT

    Backtrace (innermost frame first):
       0: (LAMBDA COMMON-LISP::C)
       1: HANDLER-BIND
       2: restart ABORT
       3: BLOCK COMMON-LISP::DONE
       4: (COMMON-LISP::%REPL-EVAL COMMON-LISP::FORM)
       5: CATCH BOCL::%QUIT-CATCH-TAG

    Available restarts:
       0: [ABORT]

Normal error handling so far.

    debug> (boc:quit) 

    In scan_symbol:
    ERROR: READ: there is no package named BOC

    ~/src/public/bocl/issues/open
    master
    13:51[pjb@thalassa org.xquartz:0 open 17Gi]$ echo $?
    65

The debugger must handle errors and call INVOKE-DEBUGGER recursively.
We cannot exit just for a small error (only crash would justify it).


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
FIXED in v1.4.38.

Root cause: in %RUN-DEBUGGER (bootstrap/prelude.lisp) only the *eval* branch of a
debugger command line was wrapped in HANDLER-CASE.  The command line is first
READ by %DEBUGGER-READ-LINE-FORMS, and that READ was unprotected — so a reader
error (e.g. `(boc:quit)` -> "READ: there is no package named BOC", a catchable
kernel ERROR()) propagated out of %RUN-DEBUGGER, past the REPL's handler-bind
(whose own cluster is disabled while its handler runs), to the kernel's unhandled
fallback, which aborted the process (exit 65).

Fix: wrap the whole read-and-dispatch body of the debugger loop in a
HANDLER-CASE for SERIOUS-CONDITION; on a hit, call INVOKE-DEBUGGER recursively on
the new condition (CLHS 9.1 nested/recursive debugging), then fall through and
re-prompt.  A small error in the debugger now opens a nested debugger level
instead of killing the process; only a genuine crash exits.  (The existing inner
handler that reports eval errors as "; Error during debugger eval" is kept.)

Verified A/B with *debug-io* bound to a string stream feeding `(boc:quit)` then
`:continue`: unmodified prelude -> "BUG (escaped): SIMPLE-ERROR ... no package
named BOC"; fixed -> "OK (reader error did not escape)".
